Subject: [PATCH 11172/11362] [outer-loop] The orphaned-test defect is live: 4
 files, one landed today

Measured by parsing each test's actual carnot import rather than guessing from
a path convention: 4 of 3,905 test files import an experiment module that does
not exist. This supersedes my earlier figure of 1776, which measured a
convention and was wrong by three orders of magnitude.

The fourth file is the finding. At 04:25Z I recorded three and called them five
days stale. A fourth appeared in the current milestone, so the conductor is
still writing tests for modules it does not commit, roughly four in five days.
Each is un-collectable from the day it lands and the sharded test phase cannot
see it.

A separate older class also exists: two verifier-diversity tests from
2026-05-29 collect fine and fail on an arity error. They are not in the orphan
count because they import by another route.

Checked before blaming my own change. I altered diversity_active, a field the
supervisor consumes, so I ran the consumers rather than only my own file: 212
passed, 4 failed, and the 4 have zero references to anything I touched. They
matched because -k diversity is broader than my change. The real consumers, the
trajectory-supervisor tests, all pass.

Filed as an operator decision rather than built: a pre-commit check refusing a
test that imports a non-existent carnot module would be cheap and exact, but it
would refuse conductor commits, and blocking the conductor is not my call.

